But we are not necessarily talking about the same version. 
If somebody finally comes up with some platform-independent format for
moving Nethack files, there's no reason to make it version-independent as
well. Shouldn't be much MORE work, especially if it is already made
patch-independent.

> From my (limited) exposure, it also seems that XML is horribly verbose. If 
> anyone *were* to use XML in a meanignful sense, I would expect the 
> save/bones file to be so large that compression would be necessary. This 
> would neatly sidestep the too-easily-human-readable issue.
> I suspect that the parsing and generation code and the memory it requires 
> at runtime would bloat the binary somewhat too.

I know XML results in bloated usage of disk and memory (I have written
Java classes for writing XML and parsing XML at work). That's why I
suggested an external import / export utility to convert between the
binary format and the transfer format, instead of changing the way the
game itself uses the files.
And it doesn't need to be pure XML.. for example, the end tags might be
shortened to simple </> instead of using </foo> to end <foo>. After all,
XML tags are supposed to be used as a stack where an end tag always ends
the last unfinished start tag pushed to the stack.

